===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|amphora||[[large]] [[wine]] [[vessel]], [[Roman]] [[unit]] of [[liquid measure]]}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἀμφορεύς||[[two-]][[handled]] [[pitcher]], [[Greek]] [[unit]]s of [[liquid measure]]}}, ultimately from {{der|en|gmy|𐀀𐀠𐀡𐀩𐀸|t=[[carried]] on [[both]] [[side]]s}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæmfəɹə/}}
* {{IPA|en|/æmˈfɔ(ː)ɹə/}} {{q|prescriptively "incorrect" but common; /ˈa-/ in Latin}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-amphora.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|amphorae|amphorai}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|_|historical}} A [[large]] [[vessel]], [[especially]] a [[thin]]-[[necked]] [[clay]] [[vat]] [[use]]d in [[Ancient Greece|ancient Greece]] and [[Ancient Rome|Rome]] for [[storing]] and [[transporting]] [[wine]] and [[olive oil|oil]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Myres|John L[inton] Myres]]|title=Handbook of the Cesnola Collection of Antiquities from Cyprus|location=New York, N.Y.|year=1914|page=76|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/handbookofcesnol00metriala/page/76/mode/1up|passage=By concurrent use of panel decoration and concentric-circle ornament, and by free employment of red paint, the fully developed style of the Geometric Period attains a wide range of rich and elaborate effects. These are best displayed on the necks and shoulders of the very large '''amphorai''' which are characteristic of this period.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:T. B. L. Webster|T[homas] B[ertram] L[onsdale] Webster]]|title=Athenian Culture and Society|location=London|publisher=[[w:Batsford Books|Batsford]]|year=1973|page=137|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/atheniancultures0000webs/page/137/mode/1up|isbn=0 7134 1982 2|passage=A complete set for a ten-man symposion (an ordinary size) with ten deep cups, ten shallow cups, two mixing-bowls, two wine-jugs, two hydriai, two '''amphorai''' would cost in the neighbourhood of forty drachmai, and that would not be an unreasonable price for a rich man for a special occasion.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Jonathan M. Hall|Jonathan M[ark] Hall]]|title=Ethnic Identity in Greek Antiquity|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|year=1997|year_published=2004|page=133|isbn=0 521 58017 X|passage=Traditionally, the style of an artefact has been separated from its function. Thus, the function of a Greek pot – defined in terms of whether it was intended for transportation (hydriai or large '''amphorai'''), storage (pithoi and some kraters), distribution (oinokhoai) or consumption (cups, skyphoi and plates) – tends to be treated separately from any painted decoration on its surface.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} A [[ancient Roman|Roman]] [[unit of measure|unit]] of [[liquid measure]] [[reckon]]ed as the [[volume]] of 80 [[ancient Roman|Roman]] [[pound]]s of [[wine]] and [[equivalent]] to about 26 [[liter|L]] although [[differing]] [[slightly]] over [[time]].
# {{lb|en|historical}} A [[ancient Roman|Roman]] [[unit of measure|unit]] of [[ship]] [[capacity]], [[similar]] to [[tonnage]].
# {{lb|en|botany}} A [[lower]] [[valve]] of a [[fruit]] that [[open]]s [[transversely]].

